 1. Huge up-front planning in hidden reasoning
  Before writing a single file I computed all 19 slugs, all file contents, all topic mappings, and checked for conflicts — all inside my thinking
  layer. That's ~5,000 tokens of work that produced no output and could have been done incrementally while creating files.

  2. This lesson has an unusual duplicate structure
  UN5GL6 has three sections with overlapping headings: ## Grammar, ## 文法, and ## Vocabulary. The first mirrors the other two almost exactly. This
   meant:
  - I had to figure out how to target only ## 文法/## Vocabulary in the Python wikilink script (couldn't just do count=1 naively)
  - Every slug and heading had to be reasoned about twice (once for Grammar, once for the target sections)

  3. The Polish-heading typo + correction loop
  The Środek transportu heading has wsiąć but I read it as wsiąić (an extra i). That generated a wrong slug, wrong vocabulary file, wrong container
   link, wrong wikilink insertion — then a full correction pass: grep the file, create correct slug file, edit container, re-run wikilink script.
  ~4 extra tool calls.

  What I should do differently next time:
  - Act sooner, plan less in advance — create the first grammar file immediately, then classify topics as I go
  - Grep the exact heading bytes before computing Polish-character slugs
  - For lessons with duplicate sections, note it upfront and explain the approach in one sentence rather than solving it silently in reasoning

